  "notes": "まねっこ is a childish, playful word for copying or imitating someone.\n\nWORD FORMATION:\n{真似|まね} (imitation) + っこ (childish diminutive suffix). Can also be written as {真似|まね}っこ.\n\nCOMMON EXPRESSIONS:\n- まねっこする: to copy, to imitate\n- まねっこしないで！: stop copying me!\n- まねっこ{遊|あそ}び: imitation play\n\nNUANCE:\nAlmost exclusively used by or about children. When a child says まねっこしないで!, it's a mild, teasing complaint. In parenting contexts, まねっこ is viewed positively — toddlers learn through imitation.\n\nRELATED WORDS:\n- {真似|まね}: imitation (neutral, all-ages)\n- {真似|まね}する: to imitate (general use)\n- まねっこ: copycat (childish, playful)\n- {模倣|もほう}: imitation (formal, academic)",
